# Presentation-golden corpus manifest (the fanned-out bk2 set).
#
# presentation-corpus.sh captures, for each bk2, a canonical leg (VBL-keyed) and
# an intermediate leg (deterministic alpha=0.5) on the native -vk path under
# llvmpipe, at 16:9, across both LOD modes.
#
# Fields (pipe-separated):
#   name      bk2 basename under games/spyro-1/bk2/ (its .args sidecar is
#             auto-spliced by the binary, so --skip-to-level etc. need not be
#             repeated here; the corpus overrides aspect/LOD explicitly),
#             optionally followed by `@<variant>`. The whole name is the file
#             key, so a variant row's tiles are
#             <name>@<variant>__canon__<f>-vk.png and never collide with the
#             plain row's; the bk2 is everything before the first `@`, so a
#             variant reuses the same movie under a different `launch` field.
#   start     first VBL frame to sample: the first frame %interval==0 AFTER the
#             level finishes loading + the fade settles. Every row here is a
#             --skip-to-level movie, and those all settle by ~f1700 (the level
#             loads ~f1197, GS_Playing ~f1584, fade clears by ~f1650), so 1700 is
#             the shared start. A slower-loading scene overrides it per row.
#             A start at or past the movie end samples the game running on
#             without input; the row's length is then its last pin.
#   interval  optional base VBL spacing (default 100).
#   freeze    optional 4th field: any non-empty value (e.g. "freeze") enables
#             --screenshot-freeze-backdrop for this row - needed for bk2s that
#             reach a pause / flight-results / level-exit state so the green
#             frozen-world backdrop is captured instead of a dark clear. Renders
#             an extra frame per present, so set it ONLY on freeze-reaching bk2s.
#   pins      optional 5th field: comma-separated frames that are ALWAYS sampled, on
#             top of the evenly-spaced grid and never subsampled away. The grid is
#             derived from movie length, so it lands where the arithmetic puts it and
#             routinely misses the frame a defect was actually observed at; a pin names
#             that frame. A pin is captured on BOTH legs at the SAME frame (canon@F and
#             inter@F), unlike a grid sample whose intermediate sits at F+interval/2.
#             Pins are additive - the grid's own samples keep the numbers they have, so
#             adding one leaves every existing golden tile untouched.
#   launch    optional 6th field: whitespace-separated tokens applied to every
#             leg (canon, inter, ident) of this row and to no other row. A token
#             spelled KEY=VALUE with an upper-case key is an environment entry;
#             every other token is a command-line argument appended to the run.
#             It holds a mode the plain row cannot reach - a renderer option, or
#             an env knob a capture needs - so the row measures that mode's own
#             pixels. Rows without the field launch byte-identically to before.
#             PRESENTATION_LEG_TIMEOUT=N is the harness's token: it raises the
#             row's per-leg bound to N seconds for a row that outruns the
#             default under a full corpus's load, and is not passed to the run.
#
# The frame-limit and both sample grids are DERIVED from the bk2's own length (the
# input-frame count in its Input Log.txt). The base grid steps by `interval` from
# `start`, extended one point past the movie end; it is then subsampled to at most
# OPENPETE_MAX_SAMPLES (default 5) EVENLY-SPACED canonical frames so the golden
# stays broad across bk2s without a dense per-bk2 grid. The intermediate leg
# mirrors the kept canonical frames + interval/2.
#
# The rows below cover every --skip-to-level bk2 whose movie is long enough
# for the shared 1700 start (movie > 1800 frames). UI-only / cutscene / demo /
# TAS bk2s that need a bespoke start are not in this list.
#
# name                                     | start | int. | freeze | pins | launch
